<BaseLayout title="Indigenous Peoples of North America" description="An accessible, source-led introduction to the histories, nations, cultures, sovereignty, and contemporary lives of Indigenous peoples in the United States.">
  <section class="native-hero">
    <div class="container hero-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">INDIGENOUS PEOPLES · HISTORY · LIVING CULTURES</p>
      <h1>Indigenous Peoples of North America</h1>
      <p class="lead">Many nations. Many languages. Many histories. The story of America begins long before the United States — and Indigenous peoples remain part of its present and future.</p>
      <p class="translation">История Америки начинается задолго до образования Соединённых Штатов. Коренные народы не только сыграли важнейшую роль в прошлом континента, но и продолжают формировать его настоящее.</p>
      <a class="hero-link" href="#begin">Explore the story ↓</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container native-content">
    <a class="back-link" href="/american-english-method/learn/history-heritage/">← American History & Heritage</a>

    <div class="opening" id="begin">
      <p class="eyebrow">A NECESSARY STARTING POINT</p>
      <h2>There is no single Native American story.</h2>
      <p>Long before the United States existed, North America was home to hundreds of distinct Indigenous nations and communities. They developed their own languages, systems of government, trade networks, farming traditions, technologies, art, and ways of understanding the world. Their histories were connected through diplomacy, commerce, migration, conflict, and family relationships — but they were never one single culture.</p>
      <p class="ru">Задолго до появления США Северная Америка была домом для множества самостоятельных народов. У них были собственные языки, формы управления, торговые связи, технологии, искусство и традиции. Их истории пересекались, но единой «индейской культуры» никогда не существовало.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="hero-figure">
      <img src="/american-english-method/images/history/native-peoples/Портрет вождя на фоне прерий.png" alt="Illustrative portrait of a Native American man wearing richly decorated Plains-style feathered regalia" loading="eager" />
      <figcaption>Illustrative artwork inspired by Plains-style feathered regalia. It is not a portrait of a named person or a representation of all Indigenous nations. Regalia, clothing, and cultural protocols differ among Native communities.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">WORDS AND IDENTITY</p>
      <h2>Who are Indigenous peoples?</h2>
      <p>In the United States, readers may encounter the terms <em>Native American</em>, <em>American Indian</em>, <em>Alaska Native</em>, and <em>Indigenous</em>. Preferences differ among individuals and nations. When possible, use the name a person or community uses for itself — for example, Diné, Anishinaabe, Haudenosaunee, or Cherokee — rather than assuming that one broad label fits everyone.</p>
      <p>Many communities also use the word <em>Nation</em> because it recognizes their distinct political, cultural, and historical identity. A tribal nation is not simply a cultural club or an ethnic label: federally recognized tribes have a government-to-government relationship with the United States and exercise forms of self-government.</p>
      <p class="ru">В США используются названия Native American, American Indian, Alaska Native и Indigenous, однако предпочтения различаются. Если возможно, лучше называть конкретный народ его собственным именем. Многие сообщества используют слово Nation, подчёркивая свою историческую и политическую самостоятельность.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">BEFORE THE UNITED STATES</p>
      <h2>North America before colonization</h2>
      <p>Indigenous societies were not isolated or technologically static. Across the continent, people built large settlements and cities, cultivated crops, managed forests and waterways, developed sophisticated fishing and hunting systems, made ceramics and textiles, and maintained long-distance trade routes. Communities adapted their homes, food systems, and technologies to very different environments — from Arctic coasts and forests to deserts, river valleys, plains, and the Pacific Northwest.</p>
      <div class="fact-grid">
        <article><h3>Agriculture & engineering</h3><p>Maize, beans, and squash supported many farming communities. In some regions, people built irrigation systems, raised fields, terraces, and extensive earthworks.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Cities & networks</h3><p>Places such as Cahokia were major population and ceremonial centres, connected to wider networks of exchange and influence.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Trade & knowledge</h3><p>Materials, food, tools, designs, and knowledge travelled over long distances through networks maintained by Indigenous communities.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Many environments</h3><p>There was no single way of life: coastal fishing villages, agricultural towns, mobile hunting communities, and desert settlements all had distinct histories.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="ru">Коренные общества занимались земледелием, инженерным строительством, торговлей, рыболовством и ремёслами. Существовали крупные города и развитые сети обмена. Образ жизни зависел от местной природы и не был одинаковым на всём континенте.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">A CONTINENT OF HOMELANDS</p>
      <h2>Different regions, different histories</h2>
      <p>Broad cultural regions can help us begin to understand the continent, but they are not rigid borders and should never replace the names and histories of individual nations.</p>
      <div class="region-grid">
        <article><h3>Northeast & Great Lakes</h3><p>Homelands of many Algonquian- and Iroquoian-speaking peoples, including nations of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy and many Anishinaabe communities.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Southeast</h3><p>Homelands of nations including the Cherokee, Muscogee (Creek), Choctaw, Chickasaw, and Seminole, with varied towns, farming systems, diplomacy, and trade.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Great Plains</h3><p>Many nations developed distinct lifeways shaped by grasslands, river systems, trade, and — in later centuries — the horse. Plains regalia is culturally specific, not universal Native clothing.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Southwest</h3><p>Diné (Navajo), Hopi, Pueblo communities, Apache nations, and others have deep histories in a region shaped by farming, architecture, weaving, trade, and diverse languages.</p></article>
        <article><h3>California & Pacific Coast</h3><p>Hundreds of distinct communities developed knowledge systems and lifeways connected to coastal, river, forest, and inland environments.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Northwest Coast, Alaska & Subarctic</h3><p>Coastal and northern peoples developed cultures shaped by salmon, marine resources, forests, ice, and extensive knowledge of local environments.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="ru">Региональные карты помогают увидеть разнообразие, но не должны подменять историю конкретных народов. Границы культурных областей условны и менялись со временем.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">CONTACT, COLONIZATION & RESISTANCE</p>
      <h2>Encounters that changed the continent</h2>
      <p>European arrival did not begin a single, uniform story of contact. Relationships differed by place and period. They included trade, diplomacy, military alliances, intermarriage, cultural exchange, competition, and warfare. Epidemics brought by Europeans caused catastrophic population losses in many communities. As colonial settlements and later the United States expanded, Indigenous nations faced growing pressure on their lands and political independence.</p>
      <p>U.S. history includes treaties, broken agreements, military campaigns, forced removals, confinement on reservations, allotment policies, and efforts to suppress Native languages and cultural practices. The Indian Removal Act of 1830 and the forced removals that followed are among the best-known examples. These events should be studied through both government records and Native accounts, not only through the perspective of the authorities who carried them out.</p>
      <p class="ru">Контакты с европейцами включали торговлю, дипломатию, союзы и конфликты. Эпидемии привели к катастрофическим потерям населения. Расширение колоний и США сопровождалось захватом земель, нарушением договоров, насильственными переселениями и политикой ассимиляции.</p>
      <div class="editorial-note"><strong>Historical care:</strong> Treaties were agreements involving distinct Native nations. They should not be described as if Indigenous peoples simply disappeared or voluntarily surrendered their homelands. Each treaty and removal history needs its own context.</div>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">SOVEREIGNTY & THE PRESENT</p>
      <h2>Native nations today</h2>
      <p>Indigenous peoples are not only part of the past. Native nations maintain governments, courts, schools, cultural institutions, businesses, health services, and diplomatic relationships. Artists, writers, scientists, educators, veterans, language teachers, and community leaders contribute to contemporary American life in many fields.</p>
      <p>Language revitalization is an important part of cultural continuity. Communities are developing immersion schools, dictionaries, recordings, digital tools, and programmes that help younger generations learn ancestral languages. These efforts are not simply about preserving old words: language carries knowledge, relationships, humour, history, and ways of seeing the world.</p>
      <p>As of January 30, 2026, the Bureau of Indian Affairs' directory listed 575 federally recognized Tribal entities. Federal recognition is a specific legal and political relationship with the United States; it is not a count of every Indigenous community, nor does it define who is Indigenous.</p>
      <p class="ru">Коренные народы — часть современной Америки. Их сообщества управляют собственными учреждениями, развивают образование, искусство, бизнес и программы возрождения языков. По данным BIA на 30 января 2026 года, федеральное признание имели 575 племенных образований; это юридический перечень, а не полный подсчёт всех коренных сообществ.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">LANGUAGE CONNECTION</p>
      <h2>Words from Indigenous languages in American English</h2>
      <p>English in North America absorbed words from many Indigenous languages. The histories of individual words are often complex: a term may have passed through another European language before entering English, and spellings changed over time. A word's presence in English is not a substitute for learning the language and history of the nation it came from.</p>
      <div class="word-grid">
        <article><h3><a href="https://www.etymonline.com/word/moose" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">moose ↗</a></h3><p>Borrowed from an Algonquian language of northeastern North America; the exact source form is discussed in historical dictionaries.</p><span>Animal name · Algonquian</span></article>
        <article><h3><a href="https://www.etymonline.com/word/raccoon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">raccoon ↗</a></h3><p>Recorded in English around 1600, from a Powhatan/Algonquian word.</p><span>Animal name · Powhatan</span></article>
        <article><h3><a href="https://www.etymonline.com/word/opossum" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">opossum ↗</a></h3><p>Recorded around 1600, from Powhatan opassum; the shortened form possum followed soon after.</p><span>Animal name · Powhatan</span></article>
        <article><h3><a href="https://www.etymonline.com/word/skunk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">skunk ↗</a></h3><p>From a Southern New England Algonquian source, possibly Massachusett.</p><span>Animal name · Algonquian</span></article>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note">These are introductory etymological notes. In a dedicated language lesson, each word will be checked against a specialist dictionary and its documented source language before publication.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">LEARN THROUGH TRUSTED VOICES</p>
      <h2>Explore further: museums, Native-led resources & archives</h2>
      <div class="source-list">
        <a href="https://americanindian.si.edu/nk360" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Native Knowledge 360° — National Museum of the American Indian</strong><span>Educational resources built around Native perspectives and essential understandings.</span></a>
        <a href="https://americanindian.si.edu/online-resources" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>National Museum of the American Indian — Online Resources</strong><span>Collections and learning materials on history, art, language, sovereignty, and contemporary life.</span></a>
        <a href="https://www.bia.gov/service/tribal-leaders-directory" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Tribal Leaders Directory — Bureau of Indian Affairs</strong><span>Official directory of federally recognized Tribal governments and BIA regions.</span></a>
        <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/indian-removal-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Library of Congress — Indian Removal Act Research Guide</strong><span>Primary documents, congressional records, maps, and related collections for studying removal policies.</span></a>
        <a href="https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>National Archives — Native American Records</strong><span>Government records, treaties, and archival research resources, to be read with attention to their perspective and limitations.</span></a>
        <a href="https://www.ncai.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>National Congress of American Indians</strong><span>Information and perspectives from a national organization representing Tribal Nations.</span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="ru">Мы отдаём приоритет материалам музеев, архивов, университетов и организаций, представляющих сами племенные народы. Источники государства также важны, но их следует сопоставлять с точками зрения коренных сообществ.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="learning-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">ENGLISH THROUGH AMERICAN HISTORY</p>
      <h2>Read. Listen. Understand. Discuss.</h2>
      <p>Each AEM feature will combine accessible English, Russian support, key vocabulary, historical context, visual sources, and questions for discussion. Our goal is not only to learn facts, but to understand how different peoples helped shape the country.</p>
      <p class="ru">Материалы AEM будут соединять английский текст, русский перевод, словарь, исторический контекст, визуальные источники и вопросы для обсуждения.</p>
      <div class="steps"><span>Discover</span><span>Understand</span><span>Learn English</span><span>Discuss</span></div>
    </section>
    <p class="footer-note">Editorial standard: distinguish documented history from interpretation; identify the nation and source wherever possible; avoid pan-Indigenous generalizations; and treat Native peoples as living communities with their own voices and authority.</p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>

